ed}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-3) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x - (-2)) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{4}{3} + (-3) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{13}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481501 1080 "y = 2x + 5" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481501 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481501 1083 #uuid "1605eca8-7e5d-57f4-bc3b-cada226d5c73" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481502 1080 "y = x" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481502 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481502 1083 #uuid "16744775-cdb2-5d73-b13a-213068b095ae" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481503 1080 "\\frac{2}{3}x - 1" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481503 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481503 1083 #uuid "281bcc22-655c-50c6-863e-af1edc8fb63b" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481499 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481504 1071 "![](images/ea/eaa82463dcdaad6bc1616b77c249a097483a8ca10afeda4bd7673896514b9efa.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = -3x + 1$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481504 1072 #uuid "2a4b8abf-f619-531a-a8b5-8e24db57d662" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481504 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -3x + 1\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -3x + 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-3\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-3$ that passes through the point $(0,-1)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= -3(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= -3x + (-1) \\\\\ny &= -3x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481523 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481505 1071 "![](images/5b/5baf024303863c69279bd1fb606a2bc8609dae35d5c5e1a9a998e9defaa0c621.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = 2x - 3$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481505 1072 #uuid "2b4f4747-b081-583d-87f5-e1f89a2c834b" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481505 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = 2x - 3\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = 2x - 3\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $2$ that passes through the point $(0,-5)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-5) &= 2(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= 2x + (-5) \\\\\ny &= 2x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481506 1080 "y = -3x" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481506 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481506 1083 #uuid "2cba75a6-b389-580d-9484-ecd4f0200a12" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481507 1080 "y = -x - 3" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481507 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481507 1083 #uuid "3503586c-0958-554f-b4d2-67d3c2f9dfb0" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481508 1080 "y = x - 3" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481508 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481508 1083 #uuid "3bdb9bc0-dd0c-5677-8705-434419273159" 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [17592186481509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481530 13194139535222 17592186045443 true] [